-- Biography rewrite, batch 1 (Prime Minister and four Cabinet ministers), approved 12 Oct 2026.
-- Written to the opengovt-mp-biography standard and fact-checked; each gets sources and a
-- Last reviewed date, and any review flag is cleared. Each biography is only replaced if its
-- current text is exactly the version the rewrite started from (md5 check); otherwise the whole
-- update stops and nothing changes.
\set ON_ERROR_STOP on
BEGIN;
DO $do$
DECLARE r record; cur text;
BEGIN
  FOR r IN SELECT * FROM (VALUES
    (1427, '99479859b2e4d9b22d108fa7715f3786', $b$Andy Burnham is the Labour and Co-operative MP for Makerfield and has been Prime Minister since 20 July 2026. He represented Leigh from 2001 until the dissolution of Parliament in May 2017, sat in Gordon Brown's cabinet, and then served as Mayor of Greater Manchester from May 2017 before returning to the Commons at a by-election in June 2026.

His Westminster career began in junior office as a Home Office minister from May 2005 and a health minister from May 2006. Under Brown he was Chief Secretary to the Treasury from 28 June 2007, Culture Secretary from 25 January 2008 and Health Secretary from 6 June 2009 until the May 2010 election. In opposition he held the health and education briefs and was shadow home secretary from September 2015 to October 2016. He stood for the Labour leadership in 2010 and 2015 and lost both contests.

The central decision of his mayoralty was to bring buses under public control. On 25 March 2021 he made the Greater Manchester bus franchising scheme under the Transport Act 2000. Stagecoach Manchester and Rotala brought judicial review claims; Rotala's central argument was that a fresh independent audit was required after the authority assessed the effects of Covid-19. The High Court dismissed both claims on 9 March 2022, and the Court of Appeal dismissed Rotala's appeal on 25 July 2022. Franchised services were introduced in tranches under the Bee Network name, the last on 5 January 2025. In January 2026 the combined authority said the transition had been delivered on time and within its original £134.5m budget; that is the authority's own statement, not an audited outturn. Transport Focus reported in March 2026 that overall bus journey satisfaction in Greater Manchester rose from 79 per cent to 86 per cent in 2025, the largest improvement among the areas it surveyed.

Burnham pledged to end the need for rough sleeping in Greater Manchester by 2020, and in November 2018 the combined authority launched A Bed Every Night, an emergency accommodation scheme whose second phase was backed by £6m. The single-night count fell from 268 in 2017 to 241 in 2018 and to 84 in the official 2021 snapshot, with a local count of 89. The 2020 target was not met. The figure then rose in each of the next four years, to 102 in 2022, 149 in 2023, 154 in 2024 and, according to The Mill's analysis of the government snapshot published on 26 February 2026, 197 in autumn 2025. The combined authority did not issue its usual announcement of the 2025 figure.

On housing, Burnham pledged in May 2024 to deliver at least 10,000 new council homes, with at least 1,000 in each borough and a target date of 2028. He also said he would ask the government for powers to suspend Right to Buy on those homes, and more widely where councils requested it, citing the loss of nearly 24,000 homes in the city region through the scheme in two decades. Both were commitments and requests rather than delivered results.

His national profile grew during the pandemic. In October 2020 he refused to accept the government's move of Greater Manchester into the highest tier of restrictions without more financial support for affected businesses and workers. On 20 October 2020 Boris Johnson announced that the area would move to the "very high" alert level regardless, telling the public that the mayor had not accepted the business support offered.

Burnham won the Makerfield by-election on 18 June 2026 with a majority of 9,231 on a turnout of 58.7 per cent, and was sworn in on 22 June. He was elected Labour leader unopposed on 17 July and became Prime Minister three days later.

His first decisions in Downing Street drew on his mayoral agenda. On 22 July 2026 he announced that single bus fares across England would be capped at £2 from January 2027, replacing the £3 cap, with £454m of funding, most of it from switching international climate finance to loans. On 25 July he paused changes to automatic prisoner release dates under the Sentencing Act 2026, which had been due to take effect in September, pending an urgent review. On 30 July he set out plans for English mayors to retain a share of the income tax and business rates raised in their areas, with details deferred to a white paper after the autumn budget. His first announcement as Prime Minister, on 20 July, was an additional £340m as the first phase of a five-year programme to end long-term rough sleeping, intended to provide 1,200 homes and intensive support for at least 3,000 people. Unlike his 2020 Greater Manchester pledge, it set no end date.$b$, $s$[{"title": "UK Parliament Members API: Andy Burnham biography (member 1427)", "url": "https://members-api.parliament.uk/api/Members/1427/Biography", "supports": "All government and opposition posts and dates (PUSS Home Office 10 May 2005; MoS Health 5 May 2006; Chief Secretary 28 Jun 2007; Culture 25 Jan 2008; Health 6 Jun 2009 to 6 May 2010; shadow posts incl. Shadow Home Secretary 14 Sep 2015 to 6 Oct 2016); Leigh 2001 to 3 May 2017; Makerfield from 18 Jun 2026; PM from 20 Jul 2026; Labour party"}, {"title": "UK Parliament election results: Makerfield by-election, 18 June 2026", "url": "https://electionresults.parliament.uk/elections/4555", "supports": "Burnham won, Labour hold, majority 9,231, turnout 58.7 per cent"}, {"title": "R (Greater Manchester Buses South Ltd and Rotala plc) v GMCA [2022] EWHC 506 (Admin)", "url": "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/admin/2022/506", "supports": "Stagecoach Manchester and Rotala as claimants; High Court judgment of 9 March 2022"}, {"title": "R (Rotala plc) v GMCA [2022] EWCA Civ 1048", "url": "https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewca/civ/2022/1048", "supports": "Mayor's franchising decision of 25 March 2021 under Part 2 Transport Act 2000; Rotala's audit argument; appeal dismissed 25 July 2022"}, {"title": "GMCA: Bus franchising", "url": "https://greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/what-we-do/transport/bus-franchising", "supports": "Franchise tranches including the final tranche on 5 January 2025"}, {"title": "GMCA news, 22 January 2026: final step in delivering a fully franchised bus network on time and within budget", "url": "https://www.greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/news/mayor-andy-burnham-sets-out-final-step-in-delivering-a-fully-franchised-bus-network-on-time-and-within-budget-plus-next-phase-of-improvements-for-public-transport-and-fire-and-rescue-service/", "supports": "GMCA's own claim of delivery on time and within the original £134.5m transition budget"}, {"title": "Transport Focus, Transport User Voice March 2026: bus passenger satisfaction rises", "url": "https://www.transportfocus.org.uk/?p=38708", "supports": "Greater Manchester overall journey satisfaction 79 to 86 per cent, biggest improvement"}, {"title": "GMCA news, 24 October 2019: rough sleeping down for second consecutive year", "url": "https://www.greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/news/rough-sleeping-in-greater-manchester-down-for-second-consecutive-year-mayor-pledges-further-action", "supports": "268 peak in 2017, 241 in Nov 2018; A Bed Every Night launched Nov 2018, phase 2 £6m; aim to end the need for rough sleeping by 2020"}, {"title": "GMCA news, 24 February 2022: bigger reductions in rough sleeping than England average", "url": "https://www.greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/news/greater-manchester-sees-bigger-reductions-in-rough-sleeping-than-england-average-official-figures", "supports": "2021 local count of 89 and official figure of 84"}, {"title": "GMCA news, February 2024: long-term rough sleeping", "url": "https://www.greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/news/greater-manchester-tackles-long-term-rough-sleeping-but-local-services-face-intense-pressure", "supports": "2022 figure of 102 and 2023 figure of 149"}, {"title": "The Mill, 30 May 2026: rough sleeping in Greater Manchester has risen for the fourth year in a row", "url": "https://manchestermill.co.uk/exclusive-rough-sleeping-in-greater-manchester-has-risen-for-the-fourth-year-in-a-row/", "supports": "268 (2017), 89 (2021), 154 (2024), 197 (2025); fourth consecutive rise; GMCA did not publish its usual announcement"}, {"title": "MHCLG, Rough sleeping snapshot in England: autumn 2025", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/rough-sleeping-snapshot-in-england-autumn-2025/rough-sleeping-snapshot-in-england-autumn-2025", "supports": "Publication date 26 February 2026; official snapshot source for 2025 figure"}, {"title": "GMCA news, 7 May 2024: Andy Burnham pledges 10,000 new council homes by 2028", "url": "https://greatermanchester-ca.gov.uk/news/andy-burnham-pledges-10-000-new-council-homes-by-2028-in-greater-manchester-as-he-unveils-plans-to-tackle-the-housing-crisis", "supports": "At least 10,000 council homes, at least 1,000 per borough; request for powers to suspend Right to Buy; nearly 24,000 homes lost in 20 years"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Prime Minister's statement on coronavirus, 20 October 2020", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/speeches/prime-ministers-statement-on-coronavirus-covid-19-20-october-2020", "supports": "Greater Manchester moved to Very High alert level; the Mayor had not accepted the business support offer"}, {"title": "GOV.UK, 20 July 2026: New PM launches drive to end rough sleeping at the earliest opportunity", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/new-pm-says-government-can-bring-back-hope-to-britain-and-launches-drive-to-end-rough-sleeping-at-the-earliest-opportunity", "supports": "Additional £340m first phase of five-year programme; 1,200 homes, support for at least 3,000 people; no end date"}, {"title": "ITV News, 22 July 2026: Burnham announces £2 bus fare cap from January", "url": "https://www.itv.com/news/2026-07-22/burnham-announces-2-bus-fare-cap-from-january", "supports": "£2 cap across England from January 2027 replacing £3 cap; £454m funding, about £400m from switching climate finance to loans"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library CBP-10974: Changes to automatic prisoner release dates in England and Wales (4 Sep 2026)", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10974/", "supports": "25 July 2026 pause pending urgent review; implementation had been due September 2026"}, {"title": "ITV News, 30 July 2026: Regional mayors to receive share of income tax under Burnham plans", "url": "https://www.itv.com/news/2026-07-30/regional-mayors-to-receive-share-of-income-tax-under-burnham-plans", "supports": "Plan for English mayors to retain share of income tax and business rates; details in white paper after autumn budget"}]$s$::jsonb),
    (400, '86831c1cc65f417e4ae610194de125f4', $b$John Healey has represented a South Yorkshire constituency for Labour since 1997, under three names: Wentworth, Wentworth and Dearne from 2010, and Rawmarsh and Conisbrough from 2024, when he won with a majority of 6,908 over Reform UK. Before Parliament he was a disability rights campaigner and campaigns director at the TUC. On 20 July 2026 he was appointed Chancellor of the Exchequer, five weeks after resigning as Defence Secretary in a dispute over military funding.

Under Tony Blair and Gordon Brown he was an education minister from 2001, Economic Secretary and then Financial Secretary to the Treasury from 2002 to 2007, local government minister from 2007 and housing minister from June 2009 to May 2010. The Mortgage Rescue Scheme, launched in January 2009 before he took the housing brief, ran under his oversight until the 2010 election. The National Audit Office found in 2011 that it had helped 2,600 households avoid repossession at a cost of over £240 million, against an expected 6,000 households for £205 million, and had not provided value for money.

In opposition he was shadow housing minister in 2010 and shadow health secretary from 2010 to 2011. He returned to housing in 2015, left the front bench in June 2016 and came back as shadow housing secretary that October, publishing Labour's green paper Housing for the Many in April 2018. Keir Starmer made him shadow defence secretary in April 2020.

As Defence Secretary from 5 July 2024 he oversaw the Strategic Defence Review, led by Lord Robertson and published in June 2025. The government accepted all 62 recommendations, including planned spending of £6 billion on munitions in that Parliament, at least six new munitions factories and at least £7 billion for military housing. A Defence Industrial Strategy followed in September 2025. In February 2025 the Prime Minister announced that defence spending would reach 2.5 per cent of GDP by 2027, funded by cutting aid from 0.5 to 0.3 per cent of national income, with an ambition of 3 per cent in the next Parliament; the government later committed to 3.5 per cent by 2035 under a new NATO target. NATO estimated UK defence spending at 2.3 per cent of GDP in 2025, and actual Ministry of Defence spending in 2025/26 was £62.0 billion.

He co-chaired the Ukraine Defence Contact Group with Germany and announced a £225 million military package in Kyiv in December 2024. The government stated that UK military support for Ukraine in 2025 would total £4.5 billion.

On 15 July 2025 he told the Commons of a February 2022 Ministry of Defence data breach exposing 18,714 Afghan relocation applicants, kept secret under a superinjunction until the High Court lifted it that day. He apologised, closed the Afghanistan Response Route to new offers and put its cost at £400 million. The Defence Committee reported in July 2026 that secrecy had displaced parliamentary scrutiny for nearly two years, that there had been little political decision-making on the breach between June and October 2024, and that the National Audit Office could not verify the department's later £850 million estimate. It made no finding against Healey personally.

He resigned on 11 June 2026, writing that the Defence Investment Plan settlement "falls well short of what is required for defence and the country at this dangerous time" and that he was "being forced to make decisions that would reduce the readiness of our Forces". Armed Forces Minister Al Carns also resigned. Al Jazeera reported that the settlement reached 2.68 per cent of GDP by 2030, against the 3 per cent Healey sought. The plan published on 30 June under his successor, Dan Jarvis, set out £298 billion of planned spending from 2026/27 to 2029/30, including £15 billion of additional money, of which £4.7 billion was left to be confirmed at the 2026 Budget.

Andy Burnham became Prime Minister on 20 July 2026 and appointed Healey Chancellor that day. His first announced measure, on 21 July, removed VAT from domestic electricity from 1 October 2026 at an estimated cost of £850 million in 2026-27, funded by cancelling the Digital ID programme. At Labour's September conference he said he and the Prime Minister would meet the fiscal rules but announced no Budget measures. As of 12 October 2026 he had not presented a Budget. His first was scheduled for 28 October, when the £4.7 billion of defence funding left unconfirmed in June was due to be settled.$b$, $s$[{"title": "UK Parliament Members API: John Healey biography (member 400)", "url": "https://members-api.parliament.uk/api/Members/400/Biography", "supports": "All government and opposition posts with dates (education minister 2001, Economic Secretary 2002-05, Financial Secretary 2005-07, local government minister 2007-09, housing minister 2009-10, shadow posts 2010-2024, Defence Secretary 5 Jul 2024 to 11 Jun 2026, Chancellor from 20 Jul 2026); constituency names and dates"}, {"title": "UK Parliament Members API: latest election result (member 400)", "url": "https://members-api.parliament.uk/api/Members/400/LatestElectionResult", "supports": "2024 Rawmarsh and Conisbrough result: majority of 6,908, Reform UK second"}, {"title": "National Audit Office: The Mortgage Rescue Scheme (May 2011)", "url": "https://www.nao.org.uk/reports/the-mortgage-rescue-scheme/", "supports": "Launch in January 2009; 2,600 households helped for over £240m against an expected 6,000 for £205m; not value for money"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library: Strategic Defence Review 2025, key points (CBP-10406)", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10406/", "supports": "SDR published June 2025, led by Lord Robertson; 62 recommendations all accepted; £6bn munitions, six factories, £7bn housing (planned)"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Defence Industrial Strategy 2025", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/defence-industrial-strategy-2025-making-defence-an-engine-for-growth", "supports": "Defence Industrial Strategy published 8 September 2025"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library: UK to spend 2.5% of GDP on defence by 2027", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/uk-to-spend-2-5-of-gross-domestic-product-on-defence-by-2027/", "supports": "25 February 2025 announcement by the PM; funded by cutting aid from 0.5% to 0.3% of GNI"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library: UK defence spending (CBP-8175, July 2026)", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/CBP-8175/", "supports": "2.5% by 2027, 3% next Parliament, 3.5% by 2035 commitments; 2025/26 MoD spending £62.0bn; NATO estimate 2.3% in 2025"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Defence Secretary announces £225m support package during Kyiv visit (19 Dec 2024)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/uk-leadership-on-ukraine-continues-as-defence-secretary-announced-225m-support-package-during-kyiv-visit", "supports": "£225m Ukraine package announced in Kyiv, December 2024"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: £450m surge of military support as UK and Germany chair meeting of 50 nations (11 Apr 2025)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/450m-surge-of-military-support-to-boost-ukraines-armed-forces-as-uk-and-germany-chair-meeting-of-50-nations", "supports": "Healey co-chaired the Ukraine Defence Contact Group with Germany; government's stated £4.5bn military support for 2025"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Oral statement on Afghan data breach (15 July 2025)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/speeches/oral-statement-on-afghan-data-breach", "supports": "February 2022 breach of 18,714 applicants' data; injunction lifted 15 July 2025; apology; ARR closed to new offers; £400m cost"}, {"title": "Defence Committee: Shifting heaven and earth? The Afghan data breach and resettlement schemes (30 July 2026)", "url": "https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm5902/cmselect/cmdfence/69/report.html", "supports": "Secrecy displaced scrutiny for nearly two years; little political decision-making June to October 2024; NAO could not verify £850m ARR estimate; no finding against Healey"}, {"title": "Al Jazeera: Britain's defence secretary Healey quits over defence spending (11 June 2026)", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/6/11/britains-defence-%E2%81%A0minister-healey-quits-over-defence-spending", "supports": "Resignation letter quotes; settlement reported at 2.68% of GDP by 2030 versus 3% sought (single secondary source)"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library: Defence Investment Plan, key decisions (CBP-10935)", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10935/", "supports": "DIP published 30 June 2026 under Dan Jarvis; £298bn planned 2026/27 to 2029/30 including £15bn extra, £4.7bn to be confirmed at Budget 2026; Healey and Al Carns resignations linked to earlier version"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: New PM cuts tax on household electricity bills (21 July 2026)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/new-pm-cuts-tax-on-household-electricity-bills-to-give-breathing-space-on-cost-of-living", "supports": "Chancellor Healey quoted; VAT on domestic electricity cut from 5% to 0% from 1 October 2026; estimated £850m cost in 2026-27; funded by cancelling Digital ID"}, {"title": "LabourList: John Healey's conference speech in full (28 Sept 2026)", "url": "https://labourlist.org/2026/09/john-healey-addresses-party-conference-for-first-time-as-chancellor-his-speech-in-full/", "supports": "Pledge to meet the fiscal rules; no specific Budget measures announced; Budget a month after the speech"}]$s$::jsonb),
    (3914, '7dbe60b0d1702d2e5edc72e384f26550', $b$Shabana Mahmood, a barrister, has been the Labour MP for Birmingham Ladywood since May 2010. She was Lord Chancellor and Secretary of State for Justice from 5 July 2024 to 5 September 2025, and became Home Secretary on 5 September 2025. The most consequential part of her record is the prison release and sentencing policy she set at the Ministry of Justice, parts of which Andy Burnham's government reopened in 2026.

In opposition she was a shadow home affairs, business and Treasury minister from 2010, then Shadow Chief Secretary to the Treasury from May to September 2015. Keir Starmer made her Labour's national campaign coordinator in May 2021 and shadow Justice Secretary in September 2023. At the July 2024 election she held Ladywood by 3,421 votes, or 9.4 per cent, against the independent Akhmed Yakoob.

On 12 July 2024 she announced that most prisoners on standard determinate sentences would be released after 40 per cent of their term rather than half, saying 700 places remained in the male estate. Sex offences, serious violence and several domestic abuse offences were excluded. It replaced the previous government's End of Custody Supervised Licence scheme, under which more than 10,000 people had been released early, and took effect from September 2024.

Her department launched an independent sentencing review under the former Conservative Lord Chancellor David Gauke in October 2024. When it reported on 22 May 2025, she accepted its recommendations except earlier release for extended determinate sentences. She set out an earned progression model under which standard determinate sentence prisoners could be released from one third of their sentence, with longer custody for bad behaviour, plus up to £700 million a year in extra probation funding by the end of the spending review period and £4.7 billion for prison building towards 14,000 places by 2031. These were allocations and targets, not spending outturns.

She introduced the Sentencing Bill on 2 September 2025, three days before moving to the Home Office. Her successor took it through its remaining stages, and it received Royal Assent on 22 January 2026. In October 2025 the Commons rejected, by 307 votes to 182, an opposition amendment to exclude rape, child sexual offences, grievous bodily harm and other serious crimes from the earlier release points. The Act also requires the Sentencing Council to obtain the consent of the Lord Chancellor and the Lord Chief Justice before issuing definitive guidelines.

That requirement followed a 2025 dispute. The council's revised imposition guideline, due on 1 April 2025, said pre-sentence reports would normally be considered for some groups, including ethnic, cultural and faith minorities. Mahmood said outcomes could then be influenced by race, culture or religion. The council paused the guideline, and the Sentencing Guidelines (Pre-sentence Reports) Act 2025, which received Royal Assent on 19 June, barred such guidance from referring to personal characteristics.

In July 2026 the family of PC Andrew Harper said the Ministry of Justice had told them two men convicted over his death could be released earlier. On 25 July Burnham paused the release changes pending an urgent review. The government excluded rape and certain child sexual and grooming offences on 3 August, and on 30 August added all forms of manslaughter, causing death by dangerous driving, causing or allowing the death of a child, and indecent assault. The changes came into force on 1 October 2026 for other offences. Those exclusions were made after she left the department; the original scope, without those carve-outs, was set while she led it.

At the Home Office she published Restoring Order and Control on 17 November 2025. It proposed cutting initial refugee leave from five years to 30 months, extending the wait for settlement to 20 years, replacing the statutory duty to support asylum seekers with a power, narrowing Article 8 claims and threatening visa penalties against countries that refused returns. Labour MPs including Richard Burgon and Stella Creasy criticised it in the Commons. The 30-month review applied to new adult claims from 2 March 2026; the 20-year rule had no published start date then.

Home Office statistics published on 27 August 2026 recorded 33,374 small boat arrivals in the year to June 2026, 23 per cent fewer than the 43,309 in the previous twelve months. Just over two months of that period preceded her appointment, and the figures do not show which policies caused the fall. Average occupancy rose to 65 people per boat, and September and November 2025 each recorded 71, the highest monthly averages on record.$b$, $s$[{"title": "UK Parliament Members API: Shabana Mahmood biography", "url": "https://members-api.parliament.uk/api/Members/3914/Biography", "supports": "All government and opposition posts with dates (Justice Secretary 5 Jul 2024 to 5 Sep 2025; Home Secretary from 5 Sep 2025; shadow posts 2010-2015; national campaign co-ordinator 9 May 2021; shadow Justice Secretary 4 Sep 2023); MP for Birmingham Ladywood since 2010"}, {"title": "UK Parliament election results: Birmingham Ladywood, 4 July 2024", "url": "https://electionresults.parliament.uk/elections/3302", "supports": "2024 majority of 3,421 (9.4 per cent)"}, {"title": "GOV.UK speech: New Lord Chancellor sets out measures to avert prison capacity crisis (12 July 2024)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/speeches/new-lord-chancellor-sets-out-measures-to-avert-prison-capacity-crisis", "supports": "40 per cent release point, exclusions, 700 places, end of ECSL (over 10,000 released), September 2024 start, 18-month review; byline 'Shabana Mahmood KC' (barrister)"}, {"title": "Full Fact government tracker: sentencing review pledge", "url": "https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/sentencing-review-pledge/", "supports": "Review launched October 2024, chaired by David Gauke, reported 22 May 2025; recommendations accepted except earlier release for extended determinate sentences"}, {"title": "Hansard (via Westminster Brief): Independent Sentencing Review statement, 22 May 2025", "url": "https://westminsterbrief.co.uk/archive/debate/22-may-2025/independent-sentencing-review-5877", "supports": "Earned progression with release from one third; up to £700m probation funding; £4.7bn and 14,000 places by 2031"}, {"title": "UK Parliament Bills: Sentencing Act 2026 stages", "url": "https://bills.parliament.uk/bills/4012/stages", "supports": "First reading 2 Sep 2025, later stages, Royal Assent 22 Jan 2026; Mahmood listed as sponsor"}, {"title": "Commons division 321, 21 October 2025 (TheyWorkForYou Votes)", "url": "https://votes.theyworkforyou.com/decisions/division/commons/2025-10-21/321", "supports": "Amendment 24 to exclude rape, child sexual offences, GBH and others, defeated 307 to 182"}, {"title": "legislation.gov.uk: Sentencing Act 2026, section 20", "url": "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2026/2/section/20", "supports": "Sentencing Council must get the consent of the Lord Chancellor and Lord Chief Justice for definitive guidelines"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Government to introduce legislation to block new sentencing guidelines (1 April 2025)", "url": "https://gov.uk/government/news/government-to-introduce-legislation-to-block-new-sentencing-guidelines", "supports": "Mahmood's objection to the pre-sentence reports guidance; guideline paused; bill announced"}, {"title": "UK Parliament Bills: Sentencing Guidelines (Pre-sentence Reports) Act 2025", "url": "https://bills.parliament.uk/bills/3955", "supports": "Government bill sponsored by Mahmood; enacted 19 June 2025"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library CBP-10974: automatic release changes", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10974/", "supports": "Release changes; Burnham pause 25 Jul 2026; exclusions on 3 Aug and 30 Aug; in force 1 Oct 2026; Harper case reports"}, {"title": "Express & Star/PA: Burnham to review early release policy amid concerns from family of PC Harper (22 July 2026)", "url": "https://www.expressandstar.com/uk-news/burnham-suggests-pc-harper-case-could-trigger-wider-review-of-early-release-8815330", "supports": "Harper family told by MoJ letter that two killers could be released early; Burnham's review (secondary source)"}, {"title": "GOV.UK policy paper: Restoring Order and Control (CP 1418), 17 November 2025", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/asylum-and-returns-policy-statement", "supports": "Publication date and title of asylum policy statement"}, {"title": "Hansard (via Westminster Brief): Asylum Policy statement, 17 November 2025", "url": "https://westminsterbrief.co.uk/archive/debate/17-november-2025/asylum-policy-2db0", "supports": "Content of proposals (30 months, 20 years, support duty, Article 8, visa penalties); criticism from Burgon and Creasy"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Refugee protection to be reviewed every 30 months (2 March 2026)", "url": "https://gov.uk/government/news/refugee-protection-to-be-reviewed-every-30-months", "supports": "30-month leave applied from 2 March 2026; 20-year settlement had no implementation date"}, {"title": "Home Office: Immigration system statistics, year ending June 2026, illegal entry routes", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/immigration-system-statistics-year-ending-june-2026/how-many-people-come-to-the-uk-via-illegal-entry-routes", "supports": "33,374 arrivals (down 23 per cent from 43,309); boats down 33 per cent; 65 per boat; monthly record of 71 in Sep and Nov 2025"}]$s$::jsonb),
    (420, '323f8042d87f1f3336a6c2e31df62da6', $b$Yvette Cooper has been Labour MP for the Pontefract and Castleford area since 1997, in a seat renamed and redrawn in 2010 and 2024. Before Parliament she worked as an economic policy researcher for John Smith and as an economics journalist at The Independent. Between July 2024 and July 2026 she was Home Secretary and then Foreign Secretary under Keir Starmer, and on 20 July 2026 Andy Burnham appointed her Secretary of State for Health and Social Care.

Her first government post was as a junior health minister from 1999 to 2002. She became housing and planning minister in May 2005. In July 2007, while she held the housing brief, the green paper Homes for the future set targets of 240,000 net additional homes a year by 2016 and three million by 2020; the House of Commons Library later noted that the credit crunch put those targets under serious pressure as house building fell sharply. Gordon Brown made her Chief Secretary to the Treasury in January 2008 and Work and Pensions Secretary from June 2009 until Labour lost office.

In opposition she was shadow foreign secretary from October 2010 to January 2011 and shadow home secretary until September 2015. She stood for the Labour leadership in 2015. Jeremy Corbyn won in the first round with 59.5 per cent; Andy Burnham took 19 per cent, Cooper 17 per cent and Liz Kendall 4.5 per cent. Cooper then left the front bench.

From October 2016 to December 2021, with breaks for the 2017 and 2019 elections, she chaired the Home Affairs Committee. Its July 2018 report on the Windrush generation called for compensation to be paid by the end of that year, an immediate hardship fund, a waiver of passport fees and full data on people wrongly detained or removed. The government's September 2018 response did not commit to a hardship fund, rejected the fee waiver and said compensation would follow a consultation; it reported that a review of about 8,000 removals had found 63 cases suggesting the person may have arrived before 1973. As a backbencher she also sponsored the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2019, which required the Prime Minister to seek an extension to the Article 50 period and became law in April 2019.

She returned as shadow home secretary in November 2021 and became Home Secretary on 5 July 2024. Two days later she announced a Border Security Command. On 22 July she told the Commons the Rwanda partnership was ending, saying it had cost £700 million to send four volunteers, and that asylum decisions would restart to clear the backlog. Two tranches of £75 million were announced for the command in late 2024. Official statistics for the year ending June 2025 recorded 9,100 enforced returns, up 25 per cent, alongside about 43,000 small boat arrivals, up 38 per cent, and a record 111,000 asylum claims. Cases awaiting an initial decision fell 18 per cent to 70,532, while people in asylum hotels rose 8 per cent to 32,059. Home Office data record 35,052 returns in Labour's first year, of which 9,115 were enforced and the rest voluntary; Full Fact judged a later ministerial claim that 35,000 people had been detained and removed misleading.

Her May 2025 immigration white paper proposed doubling the standard qualifying period for settlement to ten years and closing overseas recruitment to the care worker visa. In July 2025 the Commons approved, by 385 votes to 26, her order proscribing Palestine Action under the Terrorism Act 2000. On 13 February 2026 the High Court found the decision unlawful on two of four grounds: failure to follow the Home Secretary's own policy, and breach of rights to free expression and assembly. The Court of Appeal overturned that ruling on 15 June 2026, and in July 2026 the Supreme Court granted permission for a further appeal.

She became Foreign Secretary on 5 September 2025 and a week later announced £142 million in aid in Kyiv, £100 million humanitarian and £42 million for energy repairs. When the UK formally recognised the State of Palestine on 21 September 2025, Cooper called it a step to preserve the prospect of a two-state solution and said Hamas could have no role in Palestine's future.

At the Department of Health and Social Care she succeeded James Murray, who had held the post since Wes Streeting's resignation in May 2026. At Labour's conference on 30 September 2026 she announced a national collective pension scheme for care workers, promised a social care workforce plan and said Sure Start health services would return to up to 1,000 family hubs over two years. Reports of the speech gave no funding figures or start dates for the pension scheme or the workforce plan.$b$, $s$[{"title": "UK Parliament Members API: Yvette Cooper biography (member 420)", "url": "https://members-api.parliament.uk/api/Members/420/Biography", "supports": "All government, opposition and committee posts with dates (Home Secretary 5 Jul 2024 to 5 Sep 2025; Foreign Secretary 5 Sep 2025 to 20 Jul 2026; Health and Social Care from 20 Jul 2026; HAC chair 2016-17, 2017-19, 2020-21); constituency names and dates"}, {"title": "Hansard, Border Security and Asylum statement, 22 July 2024", "url": "https://hansard.parliament.uk/commons/2024-07-22/debates/FBB2B906-592F-4047-A6E1-59D6DB97573A/BorderSecurityAndAsylum", "supports": "Cooper's statement ending the Rwanda partnership, her £700 million / four volunteers claim, restart of asylum decisions, savings put into Border Security Command"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Home Secretary launches new Border Security Command (7 July 2024)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/home-secretary-launches-new-border-security-command", "supports": "Date and content of Border Security Command announcement"}, {"title": "Full Fact government tracker: Border Security Command", "url": "https://fullfact.org/government-tracker/border-security-command/", "supports": "Two £75 million funding announcements (17 Sep and 4 Nov 2024), first from Illegal Migration Act budget; Border Security, Asylum and Immigration Act passed into law 2 Dec 2025"}, {"title": "Home Office: Immigration system statistics, year ending June 2025 - summary", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/immigration-system-statistics-year-ending-june-2025/summary-of-latest-statistics", "supports": "9,100 enforced returns (+25%), ~43,000 small boat arrivals (+38%), 111,000 asylum claims (record)"}, {"title": "Home Office: How many cases are in the UK asylum system? (year ending June 2025)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/immigration-system-statistics-year-ending-june-2025/how-many-cases-are-in-the-uk-asylum-system", "supports": "70,532 cases awaiting initial decision (-18%); 32,059 people in hotels (+8%)"}, {"title": "Full Fact: Have 35,000 people been 'detained and removed' under Labour?", "url": "https://fullfact.org/immigration/shabana-mahmood-removed-detained/", "supports": "35,052 returns 5 Jul 2024 to 4 Jul 2025, 9,115 enforced, 25,937 voluntary; claim judged misleading"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Immigration white paper to reduce migration and strengthen border (12 May 2025)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/immigration-white-paper-to-reduce-migration-and-strengthen-border", "supports": "Settlement period to 10 years, closure of overseas care worker recruitment, RQF 6 threshold"}, {"title": "Home Affairs Committee: Windrush generation, Government Response (HC 1545, 4 Sept 2018)", "url": "https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201719/cmselect/cmhaff/1545/154502.htm", "supports": "Government position on hardship fund, passport fee waiver, compensation timing; 63 cases from ~8,000 removals reviewed"}, {"title": "Equally Ours: Home Affairs Committee report, The Windrush generation (July 2018)", "url": "https://www.equallyours.org.uk/home-affairs-committee-report-the-windrush-generation/", "supports": "Committee's recommendations (compensation by year end, hardship fund, passport fees, data) - secondary summary of the report"}, {"title": "UK Parliament Bills: European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2019", "url": "https://bills.parliament.uk/bills/2381", "supports": "Cooper as Commons sponsor of the private member's bill on Article 50 extension; enacted April 2019"}, {"title": "Garden Court Chambers: High Court rules proscription of Palestine Action unlawful / Supreme Court grants permission", "url": "https://gardencourtchambers.co.uk/supreme-court-grants-permission-to-appeal-palestine-action-terrorism-proscription/", "supports": "High Court ruling 13 Feb 2026 (two of four grounds), Court of Appeal reversal 15 Jun 2026, Supreme Court permission July 2026 (claimant's counsel; secondary)"}, {"title": "Jewish News: MPs vote to proscribe Palestine Action (2 July 2025)", "url": "https://www.jewishnews.co.uk/mps-vote-to-proscribe-palestine-action-as-a-terrorist-organisation/", "supports": "Commons vote 385 to 26 on the proscription order"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: New funding for Ukraine as Foreign Secretary visits Kyiv (12 Sept 2025)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/new-funding-for-ukraine-as-foreign-secretary-visits-kyiv-making-clear-ukraines-security-is-the-uks-security", "supports": "£142 million: £100m humanitarian, £42m energy"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: UK formally recognises Palestinian state (21 Sept 2025)", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/uk-formally-recognises-palestinian-state", "supports": "Recognition date and Cooper's statement on two-state solution and Hamas"}, {"title": "AOP: Yvette Cooper appointed health secretary by Prime Minister Andy Burnham", "url": "https://www.aop.org.uk/ot/news/2026/07/21/yvette-cooper-mp-appointed-health-secretary-by-prime-minister-andy-burnham", "supports": "Appointment 20 July 2026, succeeding James Murray who took the post in May 2026 after Wes Streeting resigned"}, {"title": "Nursing in Practice / Left Foot Forward: Cooper's Labour conference speech (30 Sept 2026)", "url": "https://www.nursinginpractice.com/clinical/elderly-care/plans-unveiled-for-national-pension-scheme-for-social-care-nurses/", "supports": "Care workers' collective pension scheme, workforce plan, no timeframe given; Sure Start health services in up to 1,000 hubs over two years (leftfootforward.org/2026/09/yvette-cooper-tells-labour-conference-sure-starts-back/)"}, {"title": "CIDOB: Yvette Cooper biography", "url": "https://www.cidob.org/lider-politico/yvette-cooper", "supports": "Economic researcher for John Smith (1990); economics correspondent at The Independent (1995)"}]$s$::jsonb),
    (4263, '9280d88c586135eccbea536ec82b3ff1', $b$Lucy Powell has been the Labour and Co-operative MP for Manchester Central since a by-election on 15 November 2012, which she won with a majority of 9,936 on a turnout of 18.2 per cent. Before Parliament she worked for Labour, Britain in Europe and Nesta. Between September 2025 and July 2026 she went from dismissal from the cabinet to election as Labour's deputy leader and appointment as Secretary of State for Education.

Her route into Parliament ran through Ed Miliband's leadership. She served as his deputy chief of staff and sat on Labour's executive board, stepping down in April 2012 to fight the by-election. Under Miliband she was a shadow education minister covering childcare from October 2013 and shadow Cabinet Office minister from November 2014, and vice-chair of the 2015 general election campaign, a defeat she later called "crushing and gutting".

Jeremy Corbyn made her shadow education secretary in September 2015. She argued that academies and free schools should come under local authority oversight, opposed forced academisation and said she would "fiercely contest" any revival of grammar schools. She resigned on 26 June 2016, telling Corbyn in a letter that his leadership was "untenable". Keir Starmer brought her back as a shadow business minister in April 2020, and she went on to be shadow housing secretary, shadow culture secretary from 2021 to 2023 and shadow Leader of the House.

As Leader of the House of Commons from 5 July 2024 to 5 September 2025, she ran the government's legislative timetable. On 25 July 2024 the Commons agreed to set up a Modernisation Committee under her chairmanship and to widen the definition of paid parliamentary advice that MPs may not give. At that committee's request, the Committee on Standards opened an inquiry into MPs' outside employment in January 2025. As of September 2026 it had not reported and MPs could still hold second jobs. A 2026 Constitution Society review called the Modernisation Committee slow and too close to the government. On Kim Leadbeater's assisted dying bill, a private member's bill, she told MPs in November 2024 that the government was neutral.

In May 2025, on BBC Radio 4's Any Questions, she answered a panellist who raised a documentary about grooming gangs with "Let's get that dog whistle out, shall we?" She apologised that weekend and again in the Commons on 8 May, saying: "I am very sorry for those remarks."

Starmer removed her on 5 September 2025, in the reshuffle that followed Angela Rayner's resignation, and Sir Alan Campbell became Leader of the House. In the contest for the vacant deputy leadership, the result announced on 25 October 2025 gave Powell 87,407 votes (54 per cent) to Bridget Phillipson's 73,536 (46 per cent), on a turnout of 16.6 per cent. In November 2025 she said the government should keep its manifesto pledge not to raise income tax, National Insurance or VAT, and called for the two-child benefit limit to be lifted in full.

On 25 January 2026 a Labour National Executive Committee panel voted 8 to 1 to stop Andy Burnham standing in the Gorton and Denton by-election; LabourList reported that Powell cast the only vote in his favour. After Labour finished third there in February, she said Burnham "probably would have" held the seat, while accepting collective responsibility. She campaigned in Makerfield, where Burnham won a by-election on 18 June 2026. Starmer announced his resignation on 22 June, Burnham was confirmed as leader unopposed on 17 July, and on 20 July he appointed Powell Education Secretary in place of Phillipson.

On 28 July 2026 the government announced technical education pathways from Year 10, based on Greater Manchester's baccalaureate, to be designed with mayors, colleges and employers and rolled out from September 2028, alongside changes to Ofsted inspection. No funding figure was given, and the head teachers' union NAHT called it "a direction of travel rather than detailed proposals". On student loans, Powell said the government had committed to reviewing the Plan 2 interest rate of RPI plus 3 per cent, which she had called "egregious", but that she could make no promises. At Labour's September 2026 conference she committed to creating a School Support Staff Negotiating Body and introducing vocational GCSEs alongside T Levels.$b$, $s$[{"title": "UK Parliament Members API: Lucy Powell biography (member 4263)", "url": "https://members-api.parliament.uk/api/Members/4263/Biography", "supports": "All government and opposition posts with dates (Leader of the House 5 Jul 2024 to 5 Sep 2025; Education Secretary from 20 Jul 2026; shadow roles 2013 to 2024; Modernisation Committee chair from 9 Sep 2024); Labour since 15 Nov 2012"}, {"title": "UK Parliament election results: Manchester Central by-election, 15 November 2012", "url": "https://electionresults.parliament.uk/elections/4541", "supports": "By-election date, majority of 9,936, turnout of 18.2 per cent"}, {"title": "LabourList: Ed's deputy chief of staff Lucy Powell steps down (24 April 2012)", "url": "https://labourlist.org/2012/04/eds-deputy-chief-of-staff-lucy-powell-steps-down/", "supports": "Deputy chief of staff to Miliband, seat on Labour's executive board, stepped down to fight Manchester Central"}, {"title": "Schools Week: Who is Lucy Powell? 10 facts about the new education secretary", "url": "https://schoolsweek.co.uk/who-is-lucy-powell-10-facts-about-the-new-education-secretary/", "supports": "Early career, 2015 campaign vice-chair and 'crushing and gutting', academies and grammar schools positions, Oxford claim and her spokesperson's response"}, {"title": "Schools Week: Shadow education secretary Lucy Powell quits Corbyn cabinet", "url": "https://schoolsweek.co.uk/shadow-education-secretary-lucy-powell-quits-corbyn-cabinet", "supports": "Resignation on 26 June 2016 and 'untenable' letter"}, {"title": "House of Commons Library: MPs' second jobs (CBP-10055)", "url": "https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10055/", "supports": "25 July 2024 paid-advice change and Modernisation Committee; Standards Committee inquiry from 8 Jan 2025 with no report; MPs may still hold second jobs as of September 2026"}, {"title": "Constitution Society: Parliamentary Modernisation: The Long and Winding Road", "url": "https://consoc.org.uk/blog-the-modernisation-committee/", "supports": "Criticism of the Modernisation Committee as slow and too close to the government"}, {"title": "Express & Star (PA): Minister tries to ease MP concerns over time available for assisted dying Bill", "url": "https://www.expressandstar.com/uk-news/minister-tries-to-ease-mp-concerns-over-time-available-for-assisted-dying-bill-5745886", "supports": "Powell at business questions: government neutral on the private member's bill, timetable for its later stages"}, {"title": "Irish News (PA): Lucy Powell apologises to grooming gang victims over dog whistle remarks", "url": "https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/lucy-powell-apologises-to-grooming-gang-victims-over-dog-whistle-remarks-UOZ6HG2KMZKNJNR5I3E7HFTM2E/", "supports": "Any Questions remark and Commons apology quote"}, {"title": "Express & Star (PA): Starmer makes major Cabinet reshuffle after Rayner quits over tax row", "url": "https://www.expressandstar.com/uk-news/sir-keir-starmer-makes-major-cabinet-reshuffle-after-rayner-quits-over-tax-row-5671359", "supports": "Powell sacked as Commons leader in September 2025; Alan Campbell new Leader of the House"}, {"title": "Labour Party: Deputy Leadership Result Announcement", "url": "https://labour.org.uk/updates/members-updates/labour-party-deputy-leadership-result-announcement/", "supports": "87,407 (54%) v 73,536 (46%), turnout 16.6% of 970,642"}, {"title": "Irish News (PA): Labour must stick to manifesto pledge not to raise key taxes, Lucy Powell says", "url": "https://ie.irishnews.com/news/uk/labour-must-stick-to-manifesto-pledge-not-to-raise-key-taxes-lucy-powell-says-S4JE5CDSS5MC3EID447YT5IXOA", "supports": "November 2025 positions on tax pledge and two-child limit"}, {"title": "LabourList: Andy Burnham blocked from standing in by-election by Labour NEC committee", "url": "https://labourlist.org/2026/01/andy-burnham-gorton-denton-by-election-blocked", "supports": "25 Jan 2026 8-1 vote; Powell reported as the sole vote in favour"}, {"title": "City AM: Burnham would 'probably' have won by-election, says Powell", "url": "https://www.cityam.com/burnham-would-probably-have-won-by-election-says-powell/", "supports": "Labour third in Gorton and Denton; Powell's comments and acceptance of collective responsibility"}, {"title": "GOV.UK: Ministerial appointments July 2026", "url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/ministerial-appointments-july-2026", "supports": "Powell appointed Secretary of State for Education, 20 July 2026"}, {"title": "Express & Star (PA): Schools to offer pupils technical subjects from age 14 under Burnham reforms", "url": "https://www.expressandstar.com/uk-news/schools-to-offer-pupils-technical-subjects-from-age-14-under-burnham-reforms-8824715", "supports": "28 July 2026 announcement, Year 10 pathways from September 2028, Ofsted changes, no funding figure, NAHT quote"}, {"title": "AOL (PA): New Education Secretary Lucy Powell says student loan interest rate 'needs looking at'", "url": "https://www.aol.co.uk/articles/education-secretary-lucy-powell-says-144258000.html", "supports": "Existing government commitment to a review, 'egregious', 'can't make any promises'"}, {"title": "LabourList: Deputy leader Lucy Powell addresses party conference, speech in full", "url": "https://labourlist.org/2026/09/deputy-leader-lucy-powell-addresses-party-conference-her-speech-in-full/", "supports": "September 2026 commitments: School Support Staff Negotiating Body, vocational GCSEs"}]$s$::jsonb)
  ) AS t(member_id, expected_md5, new_bio, new_sources) LOOP
    SELECT political_bio INTO cur FROM public.mp_biography WHERE member_id = r.member_id;
    IF cur IS NULL OR md5(cur) <> r.expected_md5 THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'Biography for member % has changed since the rewrite started; nothing applied', r.member_id;
    END IF;
    UPDATE public.mp_biography
       SET political_bio = r.new_bio,
           bio_sources = r.new_sources,
           bio_last_reviewed = DATE '2026-10-12',
           bio_review_reason = NULL,
           bio_review_flagged_at = NULL,
           bio_visible = true
     WHERE member_id = r.member_id;
  END LOOP;
  RAISE NOTICE 'Batch 1: 5 biographies rewritten';
END
$do$;
COMMIT;
